{
  "requirement": "Overrides the SDK’s OpenAI model, endpoint, credentials, or inference options. Omitted settings use the defaults in the agent configuration contract.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct OpenAiConfig {\n    pub model: Option<String>,\n    pub endpoint: Option<String>,\n    pub api_key_env: Option<String>,\n    pub api_key: Option<SecretString>,\n    pub options: BTreeMap<String, OptionValue>,\n}\n\nimpl OpenAiConfig {\n    pub fn new() -> Self;\n    pub fn model(self, model: String) -> Self;\n    pub fn endpoint(self, endpoint: String) -> Self;\n    pub fn api_key_env(self, api_key_env: String) -> Self;\n    pub fn api_key(self, api_key: SecretString) -> Self;\n    pub fn option(self, key: String, value: OptionValue) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for OpenAiConfig;\nimpl Debug for OpenAiConfig;\nimpl Default for OpenAiConfig;\nimpl<'de> Deserialize<'de> for OpenAiConfig;\nimpl PartialEq for OpenAiConfig;\nimpl Serialize for OpenAiConfig;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.OpenAiConfig`.\n\n**Obligations and the tests that cover them**\n- **Builders and fields.** `new` and the five builders (`model`, `endpoint`, `api_key_env`, `api_key`, `option`) must set only the requested field. `backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields` checks that `OpenAiConfig::new()` serializes to `{}`. It then applies every builder and asserts the exact JSON with model, endpoint, `api_key_env`, `api_key` and options.\n- **Serialization round-trip.** `all_backends_preserve_nested_settings_and_credentials` deserializes an OpenAI config with model, endpoint, `api_key` and nested options. It asserts the config serializes back to the same JSON and survives a string round-trip with equality. This covers `Serialize`, `Deserialize` and `PartialEq`.\n- **Omitted settings use the defaults.**\n  - `openai_inference_options_only_fill_missing_defaults` checks that `{\"backend\":\"openai\"}` deserializes to `OpenAiConfig::new()`. The default options are `max_output_tokens` 16384 and `reasoning.effort` \"low\".\n  - The same test checks that user options override or merge with those defaults: `max_output_tokens` 42, `reasoning` with only `summary`, and `reasoning` with `effort` high.\n  - `option_defaults_and_credential_resolution_are_deterministic` asserts the default model `gpt-6.1-sol` and the default endpoint `https://api.openai.com/v1`.\n  - `openai_uses_the_default_credential_variable` runs a child process with `OPENAI_API_KEY` set and asserts the default credential is resolved.\n- **Overrides take effect.**\n  - `option_defaults_and_credential_resolution_are_deterministic` shows that `api_key_env` names the variable that is looked up, because the error message contains the custom name. It also shows that an explicit `api_key` wins.\n  - `supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport` shows that the custom model and endpoint reach the HTTP request. The endpoint is a local transport and the request body carries `model` \"local-test\". It also shows that the explicit key is used.\n\n**Not separately tested**\n- `Clone`, `Debug` and `Default` are derived. They have no conditionals or transformations of their own, so the criteria exempt them from a dedicated test. `OpenAiConfig::default()` is exercised, and `new()` delegates to it.\n- `#[non_exhaustive]` is a compile-time attribute. `public_exports_match_the_appendix` covers the public surface.\n- Replacing an option with a duplicate key is tested only for `BedrockConfig`. The requirement does not call it out for `OpenAiConfig`, so I do not count it as a gap.\n\nI found no plausible requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept.",
